# SwiftFormat configuration.
#
# History worth knowing: this config had never actually run. Neither SwiftFormat
# nor SwiftLint was installed on the maintainer's machine, AND `core.hooksPath`
# was never set, so the `.githooks/pre-commit` hook that invokes them had been a
# no-op since it was written. Both are now installed in ~/.local/bin and the
# hook is wired.
#
# The rule set below is pinned to what this codebase actually is, not to
# SwiftFormat's defaults. Every rule that would have caused mass churn is
# disabled explicitly, with the reason, so `make format` is idempotent and safe
# to run at any time rather than a 416-file surprise.

--swiftversion 5.10
--indent 4
--linebreaks lf
--trimwhitespace always
--wraparguments before-first
--wrapcollections before-first
--elseposition same-line
--header strip
--exclude .build,build,DerivedData,Emuqu.xcodeproj

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trailing commas: SwiftFormat yields to SwiftLint.
#
# SwiftFormat 0.62 defaults `--trailing-commas always`, which ADDS a trailing
# comma to multiline literals. SwiftLint's `trailing_comma` rule (default
# config, active here) flags exactly that. Left alone the two tools fight, and
# running `make format` would have added ~745 trailing commas, every one of
# which SwiftLint then counts — blowing `.ci/swiftlint_warning_budget.txt` and
# turning CI red.
#
# SwiftLint wins because its position is already enforced by a committed budget
# that CI gates on. SwiftFormat's is a default nobody chose.
--trailing-commas never

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules disabled, with reasons. These are the ones that would rewrite the
# codebase rather than tidy it.
#
#   docComments             1712 sites. Rewrites `//` to `///`. The comments in
#                           this repo are its most valuable asset — they record
#                           why decisions were made, with dates. A mass
#                           mechanical rewrite of them is not a formatting
#                           change, it is a content change to the thing most
#                           worth protecting.
#
#   redundantSwiftUIGroup   189 sites. Removes SwiftUI `Group` wrappers. `Group`
#                           is not always redundant — it affects how modifiers
#                           apply to a set of views, and removing it can change
#                           layout. Not a cosmetic rule; not safe to apply blind.
#
#   wrapIfStatementBodies   2097 sites.
#   wrapGuardStatementBodies
#   wrapLoopBodies
#   wrapIfExpressionBodies
#   wrapFunctionBodies
#   wrapPropertyBodies      Force single-line bodies onto their own line. Pure
#                           churn across nearly every file, for no readability
#                           gain this codebase needs.
#
#   indent                  1585 sites. The existing indentation is consistent
#                           and readable; SwiftFormat disagrees about
#                           continuation lines specifically. Not worth the diff.
#
#   redundantSelf           427 sites. Explicit `self` is load-bearing
#                           documentation in this codebase's closure-heavy
#                           BLE/HealthKit callback code.
#
#   redundantReturn         499 sites.
#   conditionalAssignment   169 sites.
#   hoistPatternLet         152 sites.
#   redundantClosure        119 sites.
#   redundantType           30 sites.
#   preferKeyPath           55 sites.
#   andOperator             33 sites.
#   redundantViewBuilder    46 sites.  Style preferences the codebase has
#                           consistently made the other way. Changing them is a
#                           taste decision, not a cleanup.
#
#   fileHeader              181 sites. `--header strip` below already governs
#                           this; the rule additionally rewrites files that have
#                           deliberate provenance headers.
#
#   numberFormatting        163 sites. Would rewrite numeric literals in the
#                           HRV/scoring math, where the literal as written
#                           matches the cited paper.
#
#   sortSwitchCases         124 sites. Several switches here are ordered by
#                           domain sequence (recording phases, sleep stages),
#                           and alphabetising them destroys that reading.
#
#   modifierOrder           64 sites. `.swiftlint.yml` already enables
#                           SwiftLint's `modifier_order`; two tools policing the
#                           same thing with different opinions is how configs
#                           start fighting.
#
#   noForceUnwrapInTests    57 sites. Force-unwrapping in a test is a assertion
#                           that the fixture is well-formed; the failure mode is
#                           a failed test, which is the point.
#
#   Remaining disabled rules are lower-count instances of the same reasoning:
#   the codebase is internally consistent and the rule disagrees with it.
--disable docComments,redundantSwiftUIGroup,wrapIfStatementBodies,wrapGuardStatementBodies,wrapLoopBodies,wrapIfExpressionBodies,wrapFunctionBodies,wrapPropertyBodies,indent,redundantSelf,redundantReturn,conditionalAssignment,hoistPatternLet,redundantClosure,redundantType,preferKeyPath,andOperator,redundantViewBuilder,fileHeader,numberFormatting,sortSwitchCases,modifierOrder,noForceUnwrapInTests,wrapArguments,wrapMultilineStatementBraces,consecutiveSpaces,blankLinesAroundMark,blankLinesBetweenScopes,blankLinesAtStartOfScope,blankLinesAtEndOfScope,trailingSpace,spaceAroundOperators,unusedArguments,hoistTry,hoistAwait,docCommentsBeforeModifiers,redundantVariable,redundantThrows,redundantOptionalBinding,redundantFileprivate
